.\" Man page for plexdo (plexdo).
.\" Regenerate or edit alongside AI.prompt.md; the .TH version must match
.\" __version__ in src/plexdo/__init__.py.
.TH PLEXDO 1 "2026-09-19" "plexdo 1.1.23" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
plexdo \- command\-line interface for Plex Media Server
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B plexdo
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ command-options ]
.RI [ arguments ...]
.br
.B plexdo
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ command-options ]
.RI [ arguments ...]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B plexdo
builds and copies playlists, exports libraries to M3U or a static HTML photo
gallery, synchronises watched state between users, streams media to standard
output, and reports on server activity.
.PP
.B plexdo
is an alias for the same program; prefer it on Windows, where a dot in a
command name interacts awkwardly with
.BR PATHEXT .
.PP
Wherever a
.I user
is required you may give the numeric user ID or the user\(aqs title, and
wherever a
.I library
is required you may give the numeric library ID or its title. User ID
.B 0
always means the admin account. Titles are matched exactly first, then
case\-insensitively. If a value is both one entry\(aqs ID and another\(aqs title the
ID wins and a warning names the entry that was skipped; if two entries share a
title the command aborts and asks for the numeric ID.
.PP
Commands that take an existing playlist accept its name or its ratingKey
interchangeably.
.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These may be given either before or after the command name.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " \fIFORMAT\fR"
Output format: \fBtable\fR (default), \fBjson\fR, \fByaml\fR, \fBcsv\fR, or
\fBclixml\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Shorthand for \fB\-\-format json\fR.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print high\-level progress to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Print detailed internal logs to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would happen without mutating Plex.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-wide
Do not shrink table columns to the terminal width. By default the widest
column is truncated, with an ellipsis, so that a row fits on one line, and the
columns narrowed are reported at
.B \-\-verbose
level. Redirected output is never truncated, so piping to a file or to
.BR grep (1)
always carries the whole value.
.TP
.BI \-\-throttle " SECONDS"
Seconds to wait between requests in operations that query once per item, when
there are more than 50 of them. Defaults to 0.25; pass 0 to disable. Walking
every season of a large library, or writing watched state item by item, is
otherwise enough traffic to slow a server down or trip its rate limiting.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the installed version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit. Every command accepts this.
.PP
All logging goes to standard error, so machine\-readable output on standard
output is always safe to pipe.
.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.TP
.B table
Box\-drawn and width\-aligned, accounting for double\-width characters. Falls
back to ASCII box characters when the console encoding cannot represent the
Unicode ones.
.TP
.B json
Non\-ASCII is escaped, so it prints on any console.
.TP
.B yaml
Strings are always quoted, so a title such as \fBNO\fR or \fB1.10\fR survives
as a string rather than being reinterpreted as a boolean or a float.
.TP
.B csv
Header row plus one row per record, with newline line endings.
.TP
.B clixml
PowerShell CLIXML with typed properties, for \fBImport\-Clixml\fR.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Listing and searching
.TP
.B list\-libraries
List every library with its ID, type, and title.
.TP
.BI list\-titles " library " [ \-\-album " ALBUM" ]
.RB ( list\-library
is an alias.)
List the titles in a library. Table output shows the rating key, title,
release date, rating, and studio; a machine\-readable format carries every
field the server returned for each item, and for a show library nests each
show\(aqs episodes under a
.B seasons
object keyed by season name. Nested objects such as media and their parts are
expanded, so a file name is at
.BR media[].parts[].file ,
and the paths are repeated in a top\-level
.B files
list for convenience. Note that
.B sourceURI
is not a file name: it is the remote server URI of an item in someone else\(aqs
playlist, and is null for anything on your own server. \fB\-\-album\fR restricts a photo library to one
album and is ignored elsewhere.
.TP
.BI list\-show " ratingKey " [ \-\-m3u " PATH" ]
List every episode of a show, skipping specials.
.TP
.BI list\-users
List users with their ID, account type (\fBmanaged\fR, \fBhome\fR,
\fBfriend\fR, \fBshared\fR), title, username, and email.
.TP
.BI list\-playlists " user"
List a user\(aqs playlists.
.TP
.BI list\-playlist " user playlist " [ \-\-m3u " PATH" ]
List the items in one playlist, named or by ratingKey.
.TP
.BI show\-metadata " ratingKey"
Show full metadata for one item, with fields varying by media type, including
the server\-side path of every file backing it. A table names a lone file
.BR file ,
and numbers them when there are several; a machine\-readable format carries
them as a
.B files
list. Shows and seasons have no media of their own, so they list none: those
files belong to the episodes.
.TP
.BI search " user query " [ \-\-media\-type " TYPE" "] [" \-\-library\-id " LIBRARY" ]
Search as the given user. \fITYPE\fR is one of \fBmovie\fR, \fBshow\fR,
\fBepisode\fR, \fBtrack\fR, \fBphoto\fR, \fBalbum\fR, \fBartist\fR. A library
that cannot be searched is warned about and skipped rather than aborting.
.
.SS Building playlists
Playlists are always constructed fully in memory, validated, previewed, and
then created in exactly one API call.
.B \-\-dry\-run
stops after the preview.
.TP
.BI build\-interleaved " name ratingKey ..."
Round\-robin the episodes of several shows into one playlist.
.TP
.BI build\-chronological " name ratingKey ..."
Order shows and movies by original air date. An episode with no date is
estimated from the median interval between its neighbours in the same season,
and you are prompted only when that is impossible.
.TP
.BI build\-randomize " user source dest"
Shuffle a playlist into a new one, using
.BR secrets (3)
rather than a predictable generator.
.PP
All three accept
.BR \-o ", " \-\-overwrite ,
.BI \-\-m3u " PATH",
and
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix .
.
.SS Copying and modifying
.TP
.BI copy\-playlist\-all\-users " source_user source_playlist " [ \-o ]
Copy a playlist to every managed user, skipping the source user. The item list
is printed once, then each user produces a single line giving its outcome:
\fBcreated\fR, \fBreplaced\fR, \fBskipped\fR, or \fBfailed\fR. A failure for one
user does not end the run. In a machine\-readable format the outcomes are
emitted as one record per user instead.
.TP
.BI copy\-playlist\-to\-user " source_user source_playlist user dest " [ \-o ]
Copy a playlist to one user.
.TP
.BI append\-playlist " user playlist ratingKey ..."
Append items to an existing playlist.
.TP
.BI clean\-playlist " user playlist " [ \-\-include\-partial ]
Remove the watched items from a playlist, leaving the rest in their existing
order. The entries to be dropped are previewed first, numbered by their place
in the playlist as it stands.
.BI \-\-include\-partial
also drops items that are only part\-played; by default a resume point is left
alone, since it is what you are in the middle of watching. A smart playlist is
refused, its contents being defined by a filter rather than by its entries.
.TP
.BI remove\-playlist " user playlist"
Delete a playlist.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-overwrite ,
a copy whose name is taken falls back to appending
.RB \(dq " admin copy" \(dq;
if that is taken too the target is skipped with a warning and nothing is
created or deleted.
.
.SS Watched state
.TP
.BI copy\-watched " user_a user_b " [ \-1 "] [" \-l " LIBRARY" "] [" \-t " KEY" "] [" \-\-unwatch ]
Synchronise watched state and resume points between two users.
.RS
.PP
By default the more recent \fBlastViewedAt\fR wins. With
.B \-\-unwatch
an item unwatched for either user becomes unwatched for both, and where both
have progress the earliest \fBlastViewedAt\fR wins instead.
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-one\-way
Only write to the second user; never modify the first.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-library " \fILIBRARY\fR"
Restrict to one library.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-title " \fIKEY\fR"
Restrict to one item, by ratingKey.
.RE
.PP
Preview a full\-library run with
.B \-\-dry\-run
first: it compares every item both users can see.
.
.SS Exporting
.TP
.BI export\-playlist " user playlist path"
Write a playlist to an M3U file.
.TP
.BI export\-titles " library path " [ \-\-sort " ORDER" "] [" \-\-album " ALBUM" ]
Write a whole library to an M3U file, or to a self\-contained HTML gallery for
a photo library. \fIORDER\fR is \fBalpha\fR (default), \fBdate\fR, or
\fBrandom\fR.
.PP
M3U files hold the Plex server\(aqs own filesystem paths, not HTTP URLs.
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix
replaces the server\-side library root with another, for reading the export
where the media is mounted differently; separators follow the prefix, so a
Windows prefix produces backslashes. Because the library \fIroot\fR is what is
replaced, one prefix maps every library to the same place: export libraries
separately if they are mounted apart.
.
.SS Streaming
.TP
.BI read " library ratingKey"
Stream a media file to standard output, for piping into a player or
redirecting to a file. Quitting the player early is not an error.
.RS
.PP
.EX
plexdo read 3 12345 | mpv \-
plexdo read 3 12345 > episode.mkv
.EE
.PP
On Linux with a display attached but no desktop environment,
.BR mpv (1)
can draw straight to the console through the kernel modesetting driver, so
playback needs neither X nor Wayland:
.PP
.EX
plexdo read 3 12345 | mpv \-\-vo=drm \-
.EE
.PP
Run that from a virtual terminal rather than a terminal emulator, and make
sure your user can reach the DRM device, usually by being in the
.B video
group.
.RE
.
.TP
.BI find\-missing " SHOW " [ \-l " LIBRARY" "] [" \-A "] [" \-s " SEASONS" "] [" \-\-include\-specials ]
Report the episode numbers missing between the start of a season and its
highest episode.
.I SHOW
is a show title or ratingKey.
.RS
.PP
A season that simply stops early is not reported, since an unaired episode
cannot be told from a missing one.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-library " \fILIBRARY\fR"
Look only in this library, by ID or title. Narrows the search for
.IR SHOW ,
or the sweep for
.BR \-A .
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-all
Check every show rather than one named show.
.I SHOW
must be omitted.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-season " \fISEASONS\fR"
One season number, or several separated by commas, as in
.BR 1,3,5 .
Applies to a single show. Naming season 0 includes the specials.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-specials
Also check season 0 when no season is named. Specials are numbered
irregularly, so this is off by default.
.RE
.
.SS Server management
.TP
.BI rescan " [library] " [ \-s "] [" \-n ]
Trigger a library scan for new files.
.B \-s
.RB ( \-\-status )
lists active scan jobs instead and needs no library.
.B \-n
.RB ( \-\-now )
cancels pending scans on every library first.
.TP
.BI status " " [ \-\-section " SECTION" ]
Report server identity, active sessions (with the library, ratingKey, and the
file the player has open), shared users, system accounts,
reachable addresses, scans in progress, other background activity, and
scheduled tasks. A section that cannot be read is warned about and left empty
rather than losing the whole report. \fB\-\-format csv\fR and
\fB\-\-format clixml\fR are flat and require \fB\-\-section\fR.
.
.SS Setup
.TP
.BI login " " [ \-u " USER" "] [" \-p " PASS" "] [" \-c " CODE" "] [" \-2 ]
Authenticate against plex.tv and save the token.
.B \-\-password
is insecure: it is recorded in shell history and is briefly visible to
.BR ps (1).
Prefer the configuration file or the interactive prompt.
.TP
.B write\-config\-example
Write a template configuration file.
.B \-\-help
prints the exact template that would be written.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Read from
.IR ~/.local/etc/plexdo.ini .
.PP
.EX
[plex]
url = http://localhost:32400
token_path = $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/.plex.token
username = you@example.com
password = your\-plex\-password

[99]
username = bob@example.com
password = bobs\-plex\-password
.EE
.PP
Values may contain environment variables as
.B $VAR
or
.BR ${VAR} ,
and
.B ~
for your home directory. A name that is not set is left as literal text and
warned about. Interpolation is disabled, so a password may contain
.B %
freely.
.PP
.B cache_dir
in the
.B [plex]
section moves the completion cache; it defaults to the platform location shown
under FILES, and the shell completions honour the same setting.
.PP
A section named for a numeric user ID holds that user\(aqs credentials. They are
used only when the server refuses the admin\-issued token for that user, which
happens when nothing has been shared with them.
.PP
Both this file and the token file are checked at startup and a warning is
printed if either is readable by group or other. On Windows the check is
skipped, as access there is governed by ACLs.
.
.SH TOKEN STORE
.I token_path
holds a JSON object mapping username to token:
.PP
.EX
{
  "@admin": "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
  "bob@example.com": "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"
}
.EE
.PP
.B @admin
is the reserved key used when no
.B [plex] username
is configured;
.B @
cannot occur in a Plex username, so it never collides. The file is written
atomically with mode 0600. A file from an earlier version holding a single
bare token is read as the admin token and converted on the next write.
.PP
When a command meets a 401 for a user, three sources are tried in turn: the
server\-scoped token the admin can mint, a token already in the store, and a
fresh login with that user\(aqs configured credentials. A token obtained by the
last is saved, so the login happens once rather than on every run.
.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
Completions are provided for
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
and
.BR pwsh ,
covering user and library IDs and titles, rating keys, playlist names and
keys, and photo album names. Numeric IDs are shown with the title they belong
to.
.PP
Values are read from a 15\-minute cache under
.IR ~/.cache/plexdo ,
populated as a side effect of the list commands. A stale cache is refreshed in
the background, so completion never blocks; the values offered are whatever is
cached at that moment.
.PP
.B make install
installs all three. See
.B make help
for the paths in use.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure: missing configuration or token, an unknown ID, an inaccessible user,
a name collision refused without \fB\-\-overwrite\fR, or an invalid argument.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Referenced by the default POSIX \fItoken_path\fR. It is a private, user\-only tmpfs
on most Linux systems, which suits a secret, but it is cleared at logout: you
will need to run \fBplexdo login\fR again after a reboot. Point
\fItoken_path\fR somewhere persistent to avoid that.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Overrides the location of the completion cache on Linux and macOS.
.TP
.B LOCALAPPDATA
On Windows, the parent of the configuration and cache directories.
.B APPDATA
is used when it is unset.
.TP
.B TEMP
Referenced by the default Windows \fItoken_path\fR.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Not used; output is never coloured.
.
.SH FILES
Paths follow the platform. On Linux and macOS:
.TP
.I ~/.local/etc/plexdo.ini
Configuration.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/.plex.token
Default token store, mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/plexdo/
Completion cache.
.PP
On Windows:
.TP
.I %LOCALAPPDATA%\ePlexDo\eplexdo.ini
Configuration.
.TP
.I %TEMP%\eplexdo.token
Default token store. Note that this directory is not protected by file
permissions and Windows may clear it.
.TP
.I %LOCALAPPDATA%\ePlexDo\eCache
Completion cache.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create the configuration and authenticate:
.PP
.EX
plexdo write\-config\-example
plexdo login
.EE
.PP
List libraries, then the titles in one of them by name:
.PP
.EX
plexdo list\-libraries
plexdo list\-titles "TV Shows"
.EE
.PP
Build a playlist interleaving two shows, replacing any existing one:
.PP
.EX
plexdo build\-interleaved "Mixed Run" 101 202 \-\-overwrite
.EE
.PP
Preview a watched\-state sync before running it for real:
.PP
.EX
plexdo \-\-dry\-run copy\-watched Alice Bob
plexdo copy\-watched Alice Bob \-\-one\-way
.EE
.PP
Export a library for a client that mounts the media elsewhere:
.PP
.EX
plexdo export\-titles "TV Shows" ~/tv.m3u \-\-prefix \(aq\e\eNAS\emedia\(aq
.EE
.PP
Stream an episode into a player:
.PP
.EX
plexdo read 3 12345 | mpv \-
.EE
.PP
Machine\-readable output:
.PP
.EX
plexdo \-\-json list\-users | jq \(aq.[] | select(.type == "home")\(aq
plexdo status \-\-section sessions \-f csv
.EE
.
.SH NOTES
On Windows,
.B \-\-password
scrubbing is best effort: the technique that hides the value from
.BR ps (1)
has no Windows equivalent, so it stays visible in the process list. Protecting
the token file is also your responsibility there, as
.BR chmod (1)
cannot restrict it.
.
